\description{
Fits a biometric (twin) latent growth curve to repeated measures, with each person's age at
each wave as a definition variable, so growth is estimated in continuous age rather than at
fixed occasions. The growth factors (intercept, shape, and optionally a quadratic) decompose
into A, C, E (and optionally a born-together variance T). An optional time-invariant covariate
(e.g. a PRS) predicts the growth factors. The shape follows a chosen \code{link}:
linear, exponential, logistic, or Gompertz.
}
\details{
For person \code{t} at wave \code{w}, with age \eqn{Age(t,w)}:
\deqn{y = Int + Shape f(Age) [+ Quad Age^2] + e}
Only the shape loading \code{f()} changes with \code{link}: linear \code{Age} (no extra parameters);
exponential \verb{1 - exp(-r Age)} (free \code{rate} r); logistic \code{1 / (1 + exp(-r (Age - m)))}
(free \code{rate}, \code{mid} m); gompertz \code{exp(-exp(-r (Age - m)))} (free \code{rate}, \code{mid}).
\code{quadratic = TRUE} adds an \code{Age^2} growth factor to any link.
A, C, E (and T) are symmetric (co)variances over growth factors and covariates, estimated
directly (labels \code{A_2_1} etc.), or with \code{chol = TRUE} as Cholesky factors (\code{al}, \code{cl}, \code{el},
\code{tl}; labels \code{a_2_1} etc.), which keeps them positive-definite. Residuals are equated
across waves by default. Cross-person covariances: MZ twins A + C (+ T); DZ twins
\code{dzAr} A + \code{dzCr} C (+ T); siblings \code{dzAr} A + \code{dzCr} C. With \code{dzCr = .25}, C becomes D (ADE).
An invariant covariate is orthogonal to growth in A/C/E/T and linked only by regressions
\code{B} (labels \code{b_Int_on_Z1}, etc.) via \code{(I - B)^-1}. T is shared in full by co-born pairs only,
so with \code{tVar = TRUE} it is estimated only when siblings are present (a \verb{_T3} member or
\code{sibData}); otherwise it is dropped with a message. If \verb{_T3} columns exist, member 3 is
modeled as a non-twin sibling. Missing ages are set to a placeholder wave by wave with
\code{\link[=xmu_update_covar]{xmu_update_covar()}}, blanking only that wave's score. Because ages are definition variables,
saturated reference models are not meaningful: fit is -2LL, df, AIC. \code{plot()} draws
the model-implied growth curve with observed wave means per group.
}
